---

The Racket binding gives you a `Runtime` and `session` API shaped like
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/), with Racket values
([`glowcap.rkt`](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/bindings/racket/glowcap.rkt)).
The transport modules are `glowcap-iroh.rkt`, `glowcap-onion.rkt`,
`glowcap-relay.rkt`, `glowcap-tcp.rkt`, `glowcap-webrtc.rkt`, and
`glowcap-inproc.rkt`. The
[Bindings]($link.page('bindings')) page covers install, and
[modules]($link.page('modules')) has the actor vocabulary.

# The same round-trip

The [Start building]($link.page('start')) round-trip reads like this in Racket:

```=html
<!-- site-examples:dial:racket GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **iroh**
> ```scheme
> (require "glowcap.rkt"
>          "glowcap-iroh.rkt")
>
> ;; IROH-REACH-DISCOVERY resolves a bare iroh node ID without an address hint.
> (define node-id "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>")
> (define partner (iroh-locator node-id))
>
> (define runtime
>   (open-runtime
>    #:providers (list (iroh-provider (make-iroh-config IROH-REACH-DISCOVERY)))))
> ```
> **webrtc**
> ```scheme
> (require "glowcap.rkt"
>          "glowcap-webrtc.rkt")
>
> ;; Your signaling carries the offer and each candidate to the peer the locator
> ;; names, over a path you already hold, such as an OCapN capability both peers
> ;; can reach, and brings the answer and the peer's candidates back.
> (define signaling
>   (make-webrtc-signaling
>    #:offer (lambda (exchange peer sdp) (error "send the offer, return the answer"))
>    #:answer (lambda (exchange peer sdp) (error "send the answer to an inbound offer"))
>    #:candidate (lambda (exchange ice) (error "send one candidate"))
>    #:on-candidate (lambda (exchange peer sink) (error "call sink with each far candidate"))))
> (define partner (webrtc-locator "<64 lowercase hex characters from the peer>"))
>
> (define runtime (open-runtime #:providers (list (webrtc-provider signaling))))
> ```
> **noise-tcp**
> ```scheme
> (require "glowcap.rkt" "glowcap-tcp.rkt")
>
> ;; Same TCP sockets as keyed dialing; Noise_XK seals the CapTP stream.
> (define peer-key "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>")
> (define partner (noise-tcp-locator peer-key "127.0.0.1" 9099))
>
> (define runtime (open-runtime #:providers (list (tcp-provider))))
> ```
> **noise-relay**
> ```scheme
> (require "glowcap.rkt" "glowcap-relay.rkt")
>
> ;; Noise_XK seals the CapTP stream end to end through the relay.
> (define peer-key "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>")
> (define relay-url "wss://relay.example/relay")
> (define partner (noise-relay-locator peer-key relay-url))
> (define runtime
>   (open-runtime #:providers (list (relay-provider #:relay-url relay-url))))
> ```
> **onion**
> ```scheme
> (require "glowcap.rkt" "glowcap-onion.rkt")
>
> ;; glowcap starts no Tor; point the provider at an existing SOCKS endpoint.
> (define partner (onion-locator "<56-character onion service id>"))
> (define runtime
>   (open-runtime #:providers
>                 (list (onion-provider #:socks "/run/tor/socks"))))
> ```
> **keyed-tcp**
> ```scheme
> (require "glowcap.rkt" "glowcap-tcp.rkt")
>
> ;; Key designator, cleartext CapTP. Partners dial the public key; the host:port is a hint.
> (define peer-key "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>")
> (define partner (keyed-tcp-locator peer-key "127.0.0.1" 9099))
>
> (define runtime (open-runtime #:providers (list (tcp-provider))))
> ```
> **keyed-relay**
> ```scheme
> (require "glowcap.rkt" "glowcap-relay.rkt")
>
> ;; The key names the peer; the hint names the relay that can reach it.
> (define peer-key "<64 lowercase hex characters from the server>")
> (define relay-url "wss://relay.example/relay")
> (define partner (keyed-relay-locator peer-key relay-url))
> (define runtime
>   (open-runtime #:providers (list (relay-provider #:relay-url relay-url))))
> ```
> **tcp**
> ```scheme
> (require "glowcap.rkt" "glowcap-tcp.rkt")
>
> ;; Plain TCP uses a cleartext host and port.
> (define partner (tcp-locator "127.0.0.1" 9099))
>
> (define runtime (open-runtime #:providers (list (tcp-provider))))
> ```

From here, the CapTP workflow is the same for every transport:

```scheme
(define session (runtime-connect runtime partner))
(define greeter (session-fetch session "greeter")) ; a capability this peer published
(printf "~a\n" (session-send session greeter 'greet "world"))
(session-close! session)
(runtime-close! runtime)
```
```=html
<!-- /site-examples:dial:racket -->
```

The iroh tab needs
`glowcap-iroh.rkt` and a node id that [iroh discovery]($link.page('transports/iroh')) can resolve.

# Listen

`(accept (listen …))` hands back the same `session` that `runtime-connect` returns.
Keyed and Noise listeners take a 32-byte seed so the listener keeps the same
key across restarts. iroh binds a node id instead of a host and port.

```=html
<!-- site-examples:listen:racket GENERATED by `zig build gen-site-examples`; edits are overwritten -->
```
> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **iroh**
> ```scheme
> (require "glowcap.rkt"
>          "glowcap-iroh.rkt")
>
> ;; This listener is named by its node id. Partners can dial it from anywhere.
> (define server (listen-iroh (make-iroh-config IROH-REACH-DISCOVERY)))
> (printf "~a\n" (iroh-server-uri server)) ; hand this locator to partners
> (define host (iroh-server-host server))
> (define (accept-session) (iroh-server-accept server))
> ```
> **noise-tcp**
> ```scheme
> (require "glowcap.rkt"
>          "glowcap-tcp.rkt")
>
> ;; Same bind as keyed-tcp; Noise_XK seals every accepted CapTP stream.
> (define seed (make-bytes 32 7)) ; Replace with durable secret bytes.
> (define server (listen-noise-tcp "127.0.0.1" 9099 #:seed seed))
> (printf "~a\n" (server-uri server)) ; hand this locator to partners
> (define host (server-host server))
> (define (accept-session) (accept server))
> ```
> **noise-relay**
> ```scheme
> (require "glowcap.rkt"
>          "glowcap-relay.rkt")
>
> ;; Register this key at the relay; Noise_XK seals every accepted CapTP stream.
> (define seed (make-bytes 32 7)) ; Replace with durable secret bytes.
> (define server (listen-noise-relay
>                 "wss://relay.example/relay"
>                 "wss://relay.example/register"
>                 #:seed seed))
> (printf "~a\n" (server-uri server)) ; hand this locator to partners
> (define host (server-host server))
> (define (accept-session) (accept server))
> ```
> **onion**
> ```scheme
> (require "glowcap.rkt" "glowcap-onion.rkt")
>
> ;; glowcap starts no Tor. Name an already-running daemon's SOCKS (and control to host).
> (define server (listen-onion #:socks "/run/tor/socks"
>                              #:control "/run/tor/control"))
> (printf "~a\n" (onion-server-uri server)) ; hand this locator to partners
> (define service-key (onion-server-service-key server)) ; persist securely
> (define host (onion-server-host server))
> (define (accept-session) (onion-server-accept server))
> ```
> **keyed-tcp**
> ```scheme
> (require "glowcap.rkt"
>          "glowcap-tcp.rkt")
>
> ;; Key designator, cleartext CapTP. Persist the seed to keep the same key.
> (define seed (make-bytes 32 7)) ; Replace with durable secret bytes.
> (define server (listen-keyed-tcp "127.0.0.1" 9099 #:seed seed))
> (printf "~a\n" (server-uri server)) ; hand this locator to partners
> (define host (server-host server))
> (define (accept-session) (accept server))
> ```
> **tcp**
> ```scheme
> (require "glowcap.rkt"
>          "glowcap-tcp.rkt")
>
> ;; Plain TCP binds the host and port you choose.
> (define server (listen "127.0.0.1" 9099))
> (printf "~a\n" (server-uri server)) ; hand this locator to partners
> (define host (server-host server))
> (define (accept-session) (accept server))
> ```

From here, the CapTP workflow is the same for every transport:

```scheme
(host-publish host "greeter"
  (methods [(greet who)
            (format "Hello, ~a" who)]))
(let loop ()
  (define session (accept-session))
  (thread (lambda () (session-serve session)))
  (loop))
```
```=html
<!-- /site-examples:listen:racket -->
```

# A Goblins-style actor API

The verbs follow Goblins, but the vat (the container that runs your actors
one call at a time) and the wire are glowcap's, so an
existing Goblins program will not run unchanged. Inside `with-vat`, you:

- define an actor constructor with `(define (^name bcom arg ...) ...)`, return
  its `methods` behavior, and `spawn` it
- call it near with `$`, or send it eventually with `<-` or `<-np`
- observe a promise with `on`
- become a new behavior with `bcom`

To reach a partner, use `runtime-connect`, `session-fetch`, and `session-send`.

> **`$` and `<-`**, near-call and eventual-send, come straight from the
> [E language](https://web.archive.org/web/20241231230559/http://erights.org/),
> and [The modules]($link.page('modules')) page defines both. Across a session only `<-` and `<-np` work, since a partner's object
> is never in your vat.

# Async

Concurrency is Racket's own green threads and ports. `(session-serve sess)`
pumps the inbound loop, so run it on its own green thread, and `(on vow ...)`
runs a callback when the vow (Goblins's word for a promise) settles.
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers the CapTP ideas.

The complete API lives in the
[Racket binding README](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/bindings/racket/README.md).
Because Racket keeps Goblins's verbs, the
[rooms chat client](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/clients/racket/room-client.rkt)
reads almost like Goblins code.
